WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.319 --> 00:00:18.024
I imagined that when we had traveled as far south as Cape Horn, we would turn west and

2
00:00:18.064 --> 00:00:19.324
head into the Pacific Ocean.

3
00:00:20.225 --> 00:00:21.986
But Captain Nemo did nothing of the kind.

4
00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:24.687
Instead he continued to head south.

5
00:00:25.668 --> 00:00:26.989
Was he going to the South Pole?

6
00:00:27.889 --> 00:00:29.150
That would be sheer madness.

7
00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:33.172
In the meantime Ned had stopped mentioning his escape plans.

8
00:00:34.196 --> 00:00:38.820
I could see that our imprisonment was weighing upon him, and I could feel that rage was burning

9
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:39.260
within him.

10
00:00:40.321 --> 00:00:43.524
When he met the captain, his eyes lit up with suppressed anger.

11
00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:47.107
I feared he might do something that he would later regret.

12
00:00:48.028 --> 00:00:51.491
On the morning of March 14, he and Conseil came to my cabin.

13
00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:56.095
"I have one simple question for you, Professor," said Ned.

14
00:00:56.115 --> 00:00:59.357
"How many men do you think are onboard the Nautilus?"

15
00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:02.280
"I don't know, my friend," I replied.

16
00:01:02.956 --> 00:01:05.118
"Probably ten men would be enough to run it."

17
00:01:06.499 --> 00:01:10.322
I knew that Ned was thinking about how many men he'd have to overpower to take control of

18
00:01:10.362 --> 00:01:10.963
the submarine.

19
00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:13.665
"But you have to consider the other people too."

20
00:01:13.685 --> 00:01:15.467
"What other people?"

21
00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:16.588
asked Ned.

22
00:01:16.608 --> 00:01:21.892
"If my suspicions are correct, then the Nautilus houses more than just its crew.

23
00:01:22.293 --> 00:01:27.197
It also serves as a refuge for men like Captain Nemo who have cut all ties with land."

24
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:34.162
"Perhaps that's true, Master," said Conseil, "but this submarine can hold only a limited number of men.

25
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:38.044
You know the size of the vessel and the amount of air it contains.

26
00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:43.247
You also know that the Nautilus must return to the surface every 24 hours to refresh its air

27
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:47.309
supply. Can't you estimate the maximum number of men it could hold?"

28
00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:49.630
I did some quick calculations.

29
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:57.254
"There's enough air onboard to supply 625 men for 24 hours, but I can't imagine there are more

30
00:01:57.294 --> 00:01:58.635
than 60 men onboard."

31
00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:01.888
"That's still too many," said Conseil.

32
00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:06.690
Ned simply shook his head and left my cabin without saying anything.

33
00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:10.672
"Will you allow me to make one observation, Master?"

34
00:02:11.092 --> 00:02:11.712
asked Conseil.

35
00:02:11.732 --> 00:02:18.155
"We must try to understand how difficult it is for poor Ned because there's nothing for him to

36
00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:18.835
do here.

37
00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:22.157
He's not as interested in the wonders of the sea as we are."

38
00:02:22.177 --> 00:02:24.258
"I agree, Conseil.

39
00:02:24.618 --> 00:02:30.000
The monotony of life onboard must seem intolerable to him after a life of freedom and activity."

40
00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:36.814
Ned rarely reacted to anything at sea, but later that morning something happened that seemed to spark a

41
00:02:36.894 --> 00:02:37.555
change in him.

42
00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:42.157
The three of us were sitting on the platform, relaxing in the fine weather.

43
00:02:43.137 --> 00:02:48.179
The seasons are reversed in the Southern Hemisphere, which meant we were experiencing fall at a time when

44
00:02:48.219 --> 00:02:50.040
we normally enjoyed spring in Europe.

45
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.761
Suddenly Ned jumped up and shouted, "A whale!"

46
00:02:55.222 --> 00:03:00.084
"So," I teased him, "you haven't lost your ability to spot whales after all these months!"

47
00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.021
"Never, Professor.

48
00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:04.442
Once a whaler, always a whaler.

49
00:03:05.343 --> 00:03:08.105
Why, I believe there are ten whales out there.

50
00:03:08.545 --> 00:03:09.986
No, wait, twenty!

51
00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:14.109
And I can't go after them because I'm stuck on this confounded vessel."

52
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.533
Ned pounded his fist on the railing, but his eyes looked brighter than they had in weeks.

53
00:03:20.574 --> 00:03:25.337
From that point on, the Nautilus remained on the surface of the ocean, and we soon began to

54
00:03:25.377 --> 00:03:29.160
see floating pieces of ice about twenty to twenty-five feet long.

55
00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:34.158
We passed even larger pieces and finally encountered actual icebergs.

56
00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.522
Ned had fished in the Arctic, so he was familiar with its icebergs, but Conseil and I admired

57
00:03:40.542 --> 00:03:42.663
this natural phenomenon for the first time.

58
00:03:43.824 --> 00:03:50.688
Some of these icebergs were streaked with green; others resembled enormous amethysts with the light shining through them.

59
00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:57.772
The farther south we went, the more these floating islands increased both in number and size.

60
00:03:58.836 --> 00:04:06.638
The ice took surprising forms, sometimes resembling a Middle Eastern town with mosques and minarets, and then, a

61
00:04:06.718 --> 00:04:08.859
city tumbled to the ground by an earthquake.

62
00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:15.341
No matter where we were, Captain Nemo always managed to find a small trail of blue water for

63
00:04:15.401 --> 00:04:16.001
us to follow.

64
00:04:17.061 --> 00:04:21.482
One day the ice completely blocked our passage, but this didn't stop the captain.

65
00:04:22.482 --> 00:04:27.744
The Nautilus entered the brittle ice like a wedge and split it with a frightful cracking sound.

66
00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:33.362
Violent gales struck us and every part of the submarine was covered with ice.

67
00:04:34.463 --> 00:04:41.406
We traveled on until about two o'clock when, despite all the captain's efforts, the Nautilus could proceed no

68
00:04:41.486 --> 00:04:46.568
farther. We couldn't go backward either because every opening had closed behind us.

69
00:04:47.628 --> 00:04:50.790
I was on the platform with Captain Nemo when we became trapped in the ice.

70
00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.551
"Well, Professor, what do you think of this?"

71
00:04:55.152 --> 00:04:55.592
he asked.

72
00:04:56.668 --> 00:05:01.809
I wanted to say that I thought he'd been very unwise to get us into this situation, but

73
00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:02.730
I restrained myself.

74
00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.131
Instead I said, "I think we're stuck, Captain."

75
00:05:06.151 --> 00:05:09.632
"So you don't think the Nautilus can free itself?"

76
00:05:09.652 --> 00:05:15.093
"I think it'll be difficult because winter is coming, and the ice won't be breaking up for a

77
00:05:15.153 --> 00:05:15.773
long time."

78
00:05:15.793 --> 00:05:20.614
"Oh, Professor, you always see nothing but obstacles and trouble.

79
00:05:20.995 --> 00:05:25.856
I swear to you that the Nautilus will free itself—and then travel all the way to the South

80
00:05:25.916 --> 00:05:28.145
Pole!" "To the South Pole?"

81
00:05:28.585 --> 00:05:29.486
I thought in surprise.

82
00:05:30.307 --> 00:05:31.988
No one had ever reached the South Pole.

83
00:05:32.008 --> 00:05:36.812
"I will not fail," said the captain as if he were reading my thoughts.

84
00:05:37.733 --> 00:05:39.674
"We'll discover it together, you and I."

85
00:05:40.815 --> 00:05:42.797
"Sure," I said sarcastically.

86
00:05:42.817 --> 00:05:45.679
"There are no obstacles for us.

87
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.344
Let's blow up this iceberg, and if that fails, let's give the Nautilus wings to fly over it!"

88
00:05:52.505 --> 00:05:55.848
"No, not over it but under it," said Captain Nemo.

89
00:05:56.992 --> 00:06:02.217
"As I'm sure you know, most of an iceberg is underwater, but that won't hinder us because we

90
00:06:02.237 --> 00:06:03.217
can go still deeper.

91
00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:07.321
The only problem is that we must come to the surface for fresh air."

92
00:06:07.341 --> 00:06:12.405
"Yes, but the Nautilus is equipped with a metal battering ram on its bow.

93
00:06:12.865 --> 00:06:17.549
If you attack the ice on a diagonal, perhaps the shock would cause it to break up."

94
00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:20.292
"Now you're thinking like an optimist!"

95
00:06:21.052 --> 00:06:21.933
The captain smiled.

96
00:06:22.914 --> 00:06:25.176
The preparations for this daring attempt now began.

97
00:06:26.372 --> 00:06:31.516
Air was pumped into the submarine's reservoirs while ten men climbed along the sides of the Nautilus.

98
00:06:32.596 --> 00:06:36.639
Armed with pickaxes, they broke up the ice around the vessel, which was soon free.

99
00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.201
We went below and the submarine began to descend.

100
00:06:41.763 --> 00:06:47.186
Once underwater, we traveled at such high speed that I calculated we would reach the South Pole in

101
00:06:47.346 --> 00:06:48.167
forty hours.

102
00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:54.351
For most of the night, the novelty of the situation kept us at the gallery windows, but around

103
00:06:54.391 --> 00:06:55.632
two o'clock, I went to bed.

104
00:06:56.724 --> 00:07:01.226
I had not seen Captain Nemo since our conversation, but I assumed he was in the pilothouse.

105
00:07:02.246 --> 00:07:07.008
The next morning I returned to the gallery, where the instruments told me that the submarine was slowly

106
00:07:07.068 --> 00:07:09.849
ascending. My heart beat very quickly.

107
00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.991
Were we going to emerge and return to open waters?

108
00:07:14.111 --> 00:07:18.653
No! A second later a huge shock told me that the Nautilus had struck the bottom of an

109
00:07:18.733 --> 00:07:21.974
iceberg, a huge one, judging from the deafening sound.

110
00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:29.434
Several times that day the submarine tried to rise again, and every time it struck the ice, which

111
00:07:29.514 --> 00:07:32.199
lay like a thick, impenetrable ceiling above it.
